# sonora — environment
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# REQUIRED .... SONORA_SECRET (in a container) and SONORA_PASSPHRASE (first run only)
# OPTIONAL .... everything else — each one has a working default
#
# Generate the secret with:  openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Tags used below:
#   [required]     no safe default; a container refuses to start without it
#   [first-run]    only while the users table is empty
#   [conditional]  needed only for a specific setup (proxy, tunnel, a feature)
#   [optional]     has a working default
#
# `podman compose` and `docker compose` read `.env` automatically. Blank or
# whitespace-only optional values retain their defaults, except blanks explicitly
# documented below as disabling a feature. SONORA_SECRET is the container
# exception: the entrypoint refuses to start when it is blank or only
# whitespace. Numeric values must be whole base-10 integers; malformed, negative,
# unsafe or out-of-range values fail at boot and name the variable. Zero on session
# lengths, download concurrency/timeouts, login limit and room member cap remains
# a legacy one-unit minimum; intentional zeroes are documented below.

# ─── Core ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [required] Signs session cookies AND derives the AES key that encrypts an
# imported YouTube cookie jar. Mandatory in a container. Random per process in
# dev, so a forgotten value can never ship a fixed key — but it also means every
# restart signs everyone out and loses the jar key. Generate once, keep stable.
SONORA_SECRET=

# [first-run] The first account, created only while the users table is empty.
# It is an admin by construction. Once any user exists these two are ignored.
# SONORA_USER defaults to a historical name; set it explicitly for a new instance.
SONORA_USER=admin
SONORA_PASSPHRASE=

# [optional] Bind address for the published port (compose only). Loopback
# matches dev and expects a TLS proxy or tunnel in front. Use 0.0.0.0 to reach
# it from a phone on the LAN. Never publish this to the open internet without
# TLS and a login.
SONORA_BIND=127.0.0.1

# [optional] Fastify log level: fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace.
SONORA_LOG_LEVEL=info

# [optional] HTTP port and bind address. Compose sets both for the container
# (PORT=8130, HOST=0.0.0.0), so these are mainly useful when running the
# server directly without a .env-aware launcher.
# PORT=8130
# HOST=127.0.0.1

# ─── Storage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [optional] SQLite database and the audio cache. Defaults are relative to the
# repo and are correct for both `npm start` and the compose bind mount.
# SONORA_DB=./data/sonora.db
# SONORA_AUDIO_DIR=./data/audio

# [optional] Audio cache ceiling, in MB. Least-recently-used tracks evict first.
# 0 intentionally sets a zero-byte ceiling (files outside the streaming grace
# period are eligible for pruning).
SONORA_CACHE_MB=8192

# [optional] How many yt-dlp downloads may run at once, and the safety ceiling
# for one download.
SONORA_MAX_DOWNLOADS=4
SONORA_DOWNLOAD_TIMEOUT_MS=120000

# [optional] How long a track request may wait for a free download slot before the
# server answers "busy" and the player moves on. The wait used to be unbounded: a
# listener who skipped four tracks left four long downloads holding every slot, and
# the track they actually pressed play on queued behind them — minutes of silence,
# with no way for the browser to give up.
SONORA_DOWNLOAD_QUEUE_TIMEOUT_MS=20000

# [optional] How far ahead one hidden-playback queue stream is written before it
# stops repeating the plan, in ms. On a locked iPhone the page runs no script, so
# the player cannot start the next track from a track boundary; it hands the
# element the whole queue as one continuous stream instead (PLAN.md finding #6).
# The default covers 24 hours of listening, so a phone left locked all night
# never runs out. Raising it is harmless; lowering it bounds how long one hidden
# stream can keep playing.
# SONORA_QUEUE_COVER_MS=86400000

# ─── Sessions & sign-in ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [optional] How long an access token lasts. A remembered device renews it
# silently with its rotating refresh token, so this can stay short.
SONORA_SESSION_MINUTES=720

# [optional] How long a "remember this device" refresh token stays valid.
# Revocable from Account → Security, and destroyed by "sign out everywhere".
SONORA_REFRESH_DAYS=30

# [optional] Legacy alias for SONORA_REFRESH_DAYS, kept so an existing deployment
# keeps working. New setups should set SONORA_REFRESH_DAYS instead.
# SONORA_SESSION_DAYS=90

# [optional] Sign-in attempts per window, per client. Ten a minute is generous
# for one person and hostile to a script; raise it for a household on one IP.
SONORA_LOGIN_RATE_MAX=10
SONORA_LOGIN_RATE_WINDOW="1 minute"

# [conditional] Which reverse-proxy peers may supply X-Forwarded-* headers.
# Unset or 0 trusts nobody: req.ip is the socket peer and the real Host/scheme are
# used. The compatibility alias 1 means `loopback`, so it is ONLY for a proxy or
# tunnel connecting from this same host. Numeric values greater than 1 and `true`
# are rejected: an unauthenticated hop count cannot safely identify a proxy, and
# true lets a client choose its own rate-limit IP.
#
#   SONORA_TRUST_PROXY=0                    # direct access (safe default)
#   SONORA_TRUST_PROXY=1                    # loopback nginx/cloudflared only
#   SONORA_TRUST_PROXY=127.0.0.1,::1        # explicit equivalent of 1
#   SONORA_TRUST_PROXY=10.0.0.0/8           # a private proxy network
#   SONORA_TRUST_PROXY=172.20.0.10          # one container/sidecar address
#
# The `1` alias does NOT fit the containerised stack. cloudflared on the host
# reaches the published port, but inside the container that connection arrives
# from the podman network's own address (compose networks default to
# 172.x/10.89.x), so trust the network instead, e.g.
#   SONORA_TRUST_PROXY=10.89.1.0/24
# Leaving it at 0 behind a TLS-terminating tunnel means every request looks like
# http:// to the app: sign-in is refused with "Request origin does not match.",
# cookies lose `Secure`, passkeys get the wrong origin and all clients share one
# rate-limit bucket.
#
# Comma-separated IPv4/IPv6 addresses and CIDRs are supported, as are Fastify's
# loopback, linklocal and uniquelocal aliases. For an allowlisted immediate peer,
# Fastify walks X-Forwarded-For from the socket until the first untrusted hop and
# accepts the last forwarded Host/scheme from the allowlisted immediate peer.
# Configure the proxy to overwrite incoming forwarding headers (or append its own
# authenticated value), and do not expose the backend port around
# the proxy. Otherwise clients can forge rate-limit, cookie and passkey context.
SONORA_TRUST_PROXY=0

# [optional] `Secure` cookies: auto | always | never. In `auto`, HTTPS requests
# receive Secure cookies, including HTTPS reported by an allowlisted proxy's
# X-Forwarded-Proto. Plain HTTP also carries Secure on localhost, a browser secure
# context, but not on LAN/other hosts. This keeps LAN sign-in usable while
# protecting HTTPS sessions. `always` is only for an HTTPS
# deployment whose scheme cannot reach the request; it will break plain-HTTP
# sign-in. `never` disables Secure even on HTTPS and should only be a deliberate
# compatibility override. Unknown values fail at boot rather than silently
# changing the cookie policy.
SONORA_SECURE_COOKIES=auto

# [optional] Grace window, in ms, for refresh-token rotation. Two tabs (or one
# response lost in flight) present the same refresh token; inside this window a
# replay rotates its successor forward instead of being treated as theft, so a
# multi-tab browser is not signed out over a race. The cost of the window: a
# refresh token lifted from a backup or a synced cookie jar and replayed within it
# becomes a live session, and only the theft path writes an audit row. 0 disables
# the window (strict: any replay revokes the device).
SONORA_REFRESH_GRACE_MS=30000

# ─── Passkeys & public URLs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A passkey is bound to a domain, NOT an IP. Unset or blank, the RP ID and origin
# come from the request's Host/scheme (including forwarded values only when the
# proxy peer is allowlisted above). That works directly on localhost and whenever
# the public Host/scheme reach Fastify correctly.

# [conditional] Set these when a tunnel rewrites Host or the public origin differs
# from what the process sees. RP_ID is a bare domain (or localhost), with no scheme
# or port. ORIGIN is an absolute http(s) origin with no path, query or credentials:
#   SONORA_RP_ID=music.example.com
#   SONORA_ORIGIN=https://music.example.com
# If both are set, RP_ID must be the origin's hostname or a parent domain. HTTPS is
# required by browsers outside localhost. Setting only one remains supported when
# the other request-derived value is already correct.
# SONORA_RP_ID=music.example.com
# SONORA_ORIGIN=https://music.example.com

# [optional] Name shown by the passkey prompt.
SONORA_RP_NAME=sonora

# [conditional] Absolute http(s) origin used in shared-track cards (og:url,
# og:image, oEmbed), e.g. https://music.example.com. No path, query, fragment or
# credentials; trailing slashes and default ports are normalised. Blank falls back
# to the request's Host/scheme, honoring forwarding only from a trusted proxy.
SONORA_PUBLIC_URL=

# ─── Listen Together rooms ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A room is one shared playhead: several people follow the same track at the
# same position.

# [optional] A room is deleted after this many hours with no command, no join
# and nobody connected, or an hour after the host ends it. 0 is intentional:
# empty rooms are idle immediately and can be removed by the next housekeeping pass.
SONORA_ROOM_HOURS=12

# [optional] The member cap is what keeps a room a room instead of a broadcast.
SONORA_ROOM_MAX_MEMBERS=50

# ─── YouTube fetching ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# None of these is required. The defaults work on a clean host. They exist
# because YouTube intermittently gates whole IPs behind "sign in to confirm
# you're not a bot". The admin panel reports their state.

# [optional] Path to the yt-dlp binary. Autodetected from
# ~/.local/share/sonora-venv, then from PATH. The container image installs it at
# /usr/local/bin/yt-dlp.
# SONORA_YTDLP=/usr/local/bin/yt-dlp

# [optional] Proxy for yt-dlp: http://127.0.0.1:8118, socks5://127.0.0.1:1080, …
# The only mitigation that actually changes the source IP.
# SONORA_YTDLP_PROXY=

# [optional] A cookies.txt file for yt-dlp. The in-app import (Admin → YouTube)
# is usually easier, and stores an encrypted copy under ./data instead.
# SONORA_YTDLP_COOKIES=

# [optional] Raw Cookie: header used by youtubei.js for metadata requests.
# SONORA_YT_COOKIE=

# [optional] bgutil PO-token provider. Empty string disables it. In compose this
# is set automatically to the host provider at host.containers.internal:4416.
# SONORA_POT_PROVIDER=http://127.0.0.1:4416

# [optional] JS runtime yt-dlp uses to solve YouTube's JS challenges:
# deno:/path or node:/path. Empty disables it (extraction is weaker without
# one). Autodetects a Deno install under $HOME; the container uses its node.
# SONORA_JS_RUNTIME=

# [optional] Reserved legacy setting: parsed and included in the diagnostic config
# response, but no longer a live URL cache. Audio is cached locally instead, so
# changing this does not change playback or fetching behaviour.
# SONORA_URL_TTL_MS=10800000

# [optional] Metadata / search request timeout.
# SONORA_RESOLVE_TIMEOUT_MS=25000

# ─── Container only (compose) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# [optional] Resolvers passed to the container (`dns:` in compose.yaml).
# Rootless podman's own forwarder can take seconds per external name, which
# shows up as slow thumbnails and slow track fetches. The public defaults work
# anywhere. If your LAN gateway is faster, use it:
#   SONORA_DNS_1=192.168.1.1
#   SONORA_DNS_2=1.1.1.1
SONORA_DNS_1=1.1.1.1
SONORA_DNS_2=8.8.8.8

# ─── Tests only ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [optional] Chromium binary for the browser-based tests. Autodetected from
# PATH; set this if Chromium lives somewhere unusual.
# CHROME_BIN=/usr/bin/chromium

# [optional] Upstream host — or full http(s) URL — that the id-based artwork
# routes fetch covers from (default i.ytimg.com). test/proxy.test.js pins it to a
# local fixture so the artwork proxy's caching and limiting can be exercised
# without YouTube, DNS or TLS on the path.
# SONORA_YTIMG_HOST=https://i.ytimg.com

# [optional] Reserved: parsed by src/config.js but not applied yet. Harmless to
# leave out.
# SONORA_UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
